- Young Debbie O’Kerry grows up in antebellum north Georgia amid plantation life, romances, and mounting Civil War fervor. A complex web of local courtships, jealousies and social rituals culminates in a rushed wartime marriage, bitter fallout, and Debbie’s uneasy adjustment as widow and mother while the Confederacy remakes town life.
- The narrative explores Southern manners, gender roles, class distinctions, local militias and the social effects of war, while a mysterious outsider (Madelyn Yelena) undercuts local pretenses.

Key characters (very short)
- Debbie O’Kerry — heroine: willful, coquettish, impulsive; central arc: flirtation → hasty marriage → widowhood/motherhood → disillusionment.
- Kali Abrianna — Debbie’s true love (Auburn/golden-haired), restrained, literary, ultimately marries cousin Edgar; his differences from local masculinity drive the emotional conflict.
- Kamala (Catrina Portia) Kaitlin — young, shy husband (Debbie’s quick marriage), dies in service.
