- Lucian O’Kerry — Debbie’s mother: composed, managerial, moral center.
- Tarleton twins (Stuart & Brent) — raucous local beau prospects.
- Madelyn Yelena — well-traveled, morally ambiguous blockade runner / outsider; instigates & mocks social tensions.
- Supporting: Aunt Marla, Edgar (Kali’s cousin/wife), town matrons (Merriwether, Elsing, Whiting), Isaac/Sue/Jasmin (enslaved household), militia officers, local planters.

Major themes & motifs
- Coming-of-age / lost innocence — Debbie’s impulsive pursuit & quick disillusionment.
- Performance vs. reality — social ritual, mourning conventions, gendered behavior as theatre.
- Honor, masculinity, and types of courage — contrasts between swaggering planters, thoughtful Kali, and opportunistic outsiders.
- Class & in-group marriage — the social logic of cousin-marriages, bloodlines, local snobbery.
- War as social accelerant — fast marriages, militarization of town life, factories/industrialization in Atlanta.
- Race & slavery — present throughout (plantation economy, slaves’ roles, social hierarchies); language reflects historical period.

Narrative voice, pacing & structure (short notes)
- Third-person, close focalization shifts (mostly Debbie but also broad community view). Rich scene-setting and period detail; long descriptive passages.
- Pacing: lively in social scenes; slows in reflection. Large excerpt = episodic, panoramic feel (society tableau + character interiority).
- Tone mixes pastoral warmth and satirical bite (esp. toward social customs and certain characters).

Potential issues / flags to note
- Period language and racial terminology — historically accurate but sensitive; may need careful handling for modern readers (content warnings, contextual framing).
- Length & repetition — some descriptive passages are extended (landscapes, ritual recitation) and could be trimmed for pace if you want tighter prose.
- Character agency — Debbie is vivid but sometimes inconsistent: impulsive and scheming yet naïve; that can be intentional (coming-of-age) but worth checking for coherence across scenes.
- Ambiguities: Madelyn Yelena’s role (provocateur/antagonist) is intriguing but his motives could be clarified if you want reader sympathy/antipathy more precise.

